WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.920
Are you thinking of quitting your job?

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.000
Or have you quit your job?

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.240
You’re hardly alone.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.720
The world of work is seeing a reshuffle.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.960
Millions of people around the world quit&nbsp;
their jobs during the pandemic - and data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:20.240
show millions more are thinking of doing&nbsp;
so. Entire industries are finding it harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.920
than ever before to fill up vacancies. And&nbsp;
attitudes among workers themselves are changing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.520
And so people aren't forced to work 40 hours a&nbsp;
week at jobs they hate for people they don't like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.440
for jobs that they couldn't care less about.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.480
I think individuals had a lot of&nbsp;
time to sit and reflect and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.760
Is my life trajectory going&nbsp;
in the direction I want?

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.920
Not only did we lose workers, but&nbsp;
we're not getting fresh workers in.,

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:55.600
In this video, we ask: how has the world&nbsp;
of work changed because of the pandemic?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.880
And is that a better world&nbsp;
for workers - or a worse one?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.480
What do workers want? That’s all coming&nbsp;
up on this edition of Business Beyond.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.360
Now it obviously doesn’t make sense&nbsp;
to treat the world of work as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.920
monolith - as if all jobs were the same,&nbsp;
and the experience is similar everywhere.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.440
In countries like the United States, the&nbsp;
United Kingdom, Germany and several others, job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.520
vacancies have steadily increased as the supply of&nbsp;
workers appears unable to keep pace with demand.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.600
But these same countries exist in&nbsp;
the same world as those that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.760
experiencing the opposite&nbsp;
problem: record unemployment.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.560
Where millions of people found themselves jobless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.640
and unable to secure new work -&nbsp;
especially women and young people.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.160
So it almost goes without saying - but your&nbsp;
mileage may vary. That doesn’t change the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.640
fact that the pandemic has reached every&nbsp;
corner of the world - affecting life as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:02.560
know it and work as we’ve done it. And that’s&nbsp;
manifested in unique and unprecedented ways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.920
You might be working from home - or vaccinated&nbsp;
and back in the office - or alternating between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.120
the two. Or you might be considering a switch to&nbsp;
another job with conditions that suit you better.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.040
Over the last months,you’ve probably&nbsp;
seen or heard this phrase a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.560
((Great Resignation)). Here’s what that refers&nbsp;
to, straight from the person who came up with it.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:28.480
The great resignation is a spike in the number of&nbsp;
people voluntarily quitting their jobs in the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.320
that we've seen from April of this&nbsp;
year, and we have numbers through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:38.880
August. So about 6.7 million more Americans quit&nbsp;
their jobs during that time period this year

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.040
than last year, which makes sense because last&nbsp;
year we were in the middle of the pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.440
But it's even 2 million more Americans that&nbsp;
quit their jobs during that time period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:53.120
this year than in 2019, which was the&nbsp;
highest year for voluntary quits on record.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.720
And so it's a spike in resignations that is a bit&nbsp;
unexpected relative to the historical trend lines.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.280
Since he first used the phrase earlier in the year&nbsp;
as a predictor of a wave of resignations to come,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.240
it’s become ubiquitous. And&nbsp;
it’s come to mean different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.800
things depending on who is using it, and where.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.320
Healthcare workers at the forefront of the&nbsp;
fight against the pandemic were applauded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.560
around the world, but are burning&nbsp;
out. The International Council of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.920
Nurses (ICN), which represents 27 million&nbsp;
nurses in 30 countries, has warned that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.120
so-called intention to leave rate within a&nbsp;
year has doubled to as much as 30 percent.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.480
There’s also been a spotlight on service&nbsp;
industry and logistics workers who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:44.000
very often with precarious working conditions&nbsp;
to begin with, found themselves overworked and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.600
overwhelmed by surging demand after the economy&nbsp;
reopened following a period of lockdowns.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.480
When we're all in lockdown and we're&nbsp;
all buying online, manufacturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.480
goods were very much in demand. So we saw&nbsp;
a shift in workers moving away from these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.840
affected industries to the less affected&nbsp;
industries like manufacturing. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:13.040
as consumer demand shifted back to services, as&nbsp;
restrictions were lifted, there wasn't hospitality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:20.160
workers or people to work within the service&nbsp;
sector because they were very uncertain about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:27.600
the future of these jobs if there was further&nbsp;
lockdowns and they just had moved all together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:34.000
away from these. So then it left us with a huge&nbsp;
gap when consumer patterns changed once again.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.720
Others think more of workers resisting being&nbsp;
called back into the office after having worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.480
from home, and for whom anything other than a&nbsp;
hybrid model of working no longer makes sense.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:53.920
I think the great resignation is where people&nbsp;
who have enjoyed being at home more during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.720
Covid and are going back into jobs that&nbsp;
aren't offering them their flexibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:05.600
are choosing to leave and do something else. The&nbsp;
title suggests that they're resigning and going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.840
and doing nothing, which&nbsp;
I don't think is the case.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:13.760
In March this year, Microsoft released&nbsp;
its Work Trend Index 2021 Survey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:20.080
That’s a survey among around 30, 000 full-time&nbsp;
or self-employed workers across 31 markets.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.600
And it said that 41 percent of those surveyed&nbsp;
said they were considering leaving their jobs.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:29.600
Not surprising perhaps, because the pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:35.440
amplified existing challenges around work…&nbsp;
in other words it became even harder TO work.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:41.280
You might have had this in your workplace. You&nbsp;
might have, for various pandemic-related reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:51.200
fewer people to work with, leaving the&nbsp;
rest of you with a bigger workload than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:51.980
you would normally have, which leads to&nbsp;
more of your co-workers burning out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:52.177
then leaving. Which leaves even fewer&nbsp;
people behind to carry the even bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.177 --> 00:05:53.200
workload which leads to more of&nbsp;
your co-workers leaving and so on.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.240
So the pandemic was unique&nbsp;
in that it kind of burned out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:59.920
almost everybody in some ways. And we&nbsp;
know burnout is a predictor of turnover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.520
but the front line workers who were in health&nbsp;
care, retail food service during the pandemic were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.400
reporting high levels of burnout. Organizational&nbsp;
managers and executives who were leading their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.560
organizations through the pandemic were really&nbsp;
stressed. And although there's a lot of upsides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.960
to remote work, there were many caregivers&nbsp;
who were juggling responsibilities personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.920
professionally, child care and so forth&nbsp;
that were reporting high levels of burnout.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.560
At the same time, border closures and&nbsp;
covid restrictions hampered migration

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:31.760
- meaning there were just fewer workers around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:36.800
especially in industrialized nations,&nbsp;
as it became harder to travel to them.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:42.880
We've seen quite a lot of disruption to migrant&nbsp;
employees. So a lot of countries around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:49.600
world are very dependent on migrant labor to be&nbsp;
able to conduct the activities that they want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:57.600
conduct. And so at the start of the&nbsp;
pandemic, we saw outflows of migrant workers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:05.440
But we also have seen across the pandemic&nbsp;
disruptions, continued disruption to the inflows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:12.960
of migrant workers. So not only did we lose&nbsp;
workers, but we're not getting fresh workers in…

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.720
And that means companies are having to dig deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:20.720
into their pockets to retain people&nbsp;
- or recruit them in the first place.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:29.440
So the major impact of labor shortages is the&nbsp;
upward pressure on wage growth. So that is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:36.320
immediate impact of the labor shortage that we are&nbsp;
seeing. We see many many companies are having to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:49.440
bring hiring large bonuses to lure people to the&nbsp;
professions or just to find any workers at all.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:54.640
That’s good news for workers - and in fact,&nbsp;
the current moment of labor shortages and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:58.480
elevated resignations are being&nbsp;
framed as a moment where in-demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:04.000
workers find themselves with more leverage,&nbsp;
bargaining power, and motivation to organize.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:09.360
Workers at Starbucks for example, voted&nbsp;
to unionize for the first time in the US.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.360
That has triggered hopes of a revitalized&nbsp;
labor movement in the country.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.640
But some experts fear that&nbsp;
not all the positive gains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:20.480
may stick - rising wages for&nbsp;
one, may eventually level off.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.280
If they are granted more wages, where is that&nbsp;
passed on to?//The data at the moment, given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:29.200
the levels of inflation that we're experiencing,&nbsp;
suggests it's being passed on to the customers.//&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.640
Once we move out of the transition phase&nbsp;
that we're in, into something more permanent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.080
everything suggests that more people&nbsp;
are actually going to be at home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.960
so probably a lesser demand for the services that&nbsp;
are experiencing the wage increases at the moment.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:45.360
Worth noting that not everything&nbsp;
is about pay - the pandemic forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.360
changes in the shape of working life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.560
enabled by technology - and not everyone&nbsp;
is willing to revert to how it used to be.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:59.600
Here in Europe, a survey of 14,000&nbsp;
people from December showed that only 14%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.240
of European workers want to come&nbsp;
back to in-office work from 9 to 5.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:09.040
Experts predict that that will mean&nbsp;
companies who do want physical presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:14.240
may have to pay extra for it - but that in&nbsp;
turn could have unintended consequences.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:19.840
Some companies who can afford to pay will give&nbsp;
compensating differentials just to bring people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:25.760
back into the office// Right. So if you think this&nbsp;
process through, it’s the women who are leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:30.720
organizations that value presenteeism and being in&nbsp;
the office, are the ones that value flexibility in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.960
hybrid working, and they find a home in another&nbsp;
company, probably with less pay because you pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:39.760
a compensating differential for hybrid working.&nbsp;
Firstly, the gender gap will widen, and we wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:44.640
why, and nobody will really be able to nail this&nbsp;
down because it's impossible to nail down in data.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:49.200
In other words - some workers, say&nbsp;
working mothers or disabled people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:54.640
might value flexibility over money. They might&nbsp;
not stay in companies that would pay to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:59.680
their employees in the office, preferring&nbsp;
to take the pay cut. That could exacerbate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:04.400
wage divides and make companies with more&nbsp;
traditional working models less diverse.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:09.680
But it’s not just how much or how&nbsp;
little flexibility within a job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:12.880
that has become more important&nbsp;
to workers over the pandemic.

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:17.520
For many people the pandemic&nbsp;
also triggered an internal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:22.880
re-configuring of values - and placing where&nbsp;
work belongs in that new configuration.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:27.120
Do I need to be in the office all&nbsp;
the time, or do I even need to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:33.360
such an extreme dependent kind of job or&nbsp;
a job that takes a lot of the time? So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.720
yes, we certainly are seeing the&nbsp;
impact of labor availability if people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:47.120
are just moving away from different job choices&nbsp;
or that they are just retiring early. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:54.160
certainly something we've seen in many countries&nbsp;
that we've got an aging workforce as it is anyway.

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:58.960
I think individuals had a lot of time to sit and&nbsp;
reflect and say, Is my life trajectory going in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:03.120
the direction I want? Am I happy? Do I have a&nbsp;
sense of purpose?// Someone else coined the term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:08.720
pandemic epiphanies, and I thought it was a good&nbsp;
one to capture how people were reassessing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:14.320
place of work in their lives, deciding I want to&nbsp;
spend more time with family after the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:19.280
I want to spend more time pursuing my hobbies&nbsp;
and other aspects of myself outside of work.

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:25.040
In other words - the pandemic has gotten&nbsp;
people asking themselves why they’re alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:29.440
and for some, “mostly to work” isn’t&nbsp;
really the answer they’ve come up with.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:35.280
You might have heard of the r/Antiwork subreddit -&nbsp;
that’s a subreddit for those who want to end work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.720
are curious about ending work, want to get&nbsp;
the most out of a work-free life, want more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:42.960
information on anti-work ideas and want personal&nbsp;
help with their own jobs/work-related struggles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:46.000
They saw membership surge this year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.520
with posts relating to quitting&nbsp;
jobs being especially popular.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:55.840
To give a context to this, we&nbsp;
had around 189,000 last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:04.400
in October of last year, we now have 1.2 million&nbsp;
people. So the uptick has been incredible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:10.880
I think uptick is probably under-selling&nbsp;
it as a moderator. But yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:17.840
I was very surprised when all this was&nbsp;
happening, but at the same time, I wasn't.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:18.240
Here’s a recurring meme:

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:19.680
Q: What’s your dream job?

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:25.600
A: I don’t dream of labor.

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:32.320
The pandemic has forced a discussion on the&nbsp;
value of labor, but not just in a transactional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:38.240
sense - it’s helped accelerate a rethink of&nbsp;
society’s relationship with work. Some have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:43.120
pointed out - there’s a historical precedent&nbsp;
to a pandemic triggering a systemic overhaul.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:48.800
The black death - or the plague&nbsp;
- effectively ended feudalism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:53.520
A drastically reduced population meant&nbsp;
there was less labor to go around. Serfs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:57.360
found themselves in demand, and less&nbsp;
bound to the estates employing them.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:01.440
….and the gap between have-nots like&nbsp;
peasants and haves like landlords&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:03.840
eventually became less and less defined.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:08.480
That’s obviously radically oversimplified.&nbsp;
And not exactly what is happening now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:12.480
But the re-examination of hustle&nbsp;
culture in countries like the US,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:17.440
or the rise of the so-called lying flat&nbsp;
movement in China indicates that the notion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:23.360
of work as a good in itself is not as obvious&nbsp;
as it once was - as rising housing prices and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:28.480
recurring financial crises make the gains&nbsp;
from dedicated workaholism less apparent.

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:32.160
To me, anti-work is a philosophy that is aiming to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:37.760
ideally reduce to zero as much&nbsp;
coercive work as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:43.840
That coercive element of it is really the most&nbsp;
important part. And so people aren't forced

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:54.160
don't like for jobs that they couldn't care&nbsp;
less about. And for wages that are unfair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:57.760
usually in bad environments.

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:00.400
But the anti-work movement isn’t exactly new.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.800
In an essay called the Abolition&nbsp;
of Work by Bob Black in 1985.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:06.960
No one should ever work.

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:13.280
Work is the source of nearly all the misery in&nbsp;
the world. Almost any evil you’d care to name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:16.880
comes from working or from living&nbsp;
in a world designed for work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:20.160
In order to stop suffering,&nbsp;
we have to stop working.

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:27.120
Maybe NOT all the misery. Or ANY evil. In any&nbsp;
case, stopping work isn’t really an option for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:32.240
that many people. But stopping suffering&nbsp;
- and promoting wellbeing - definitely is.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:36.720
There’s more to life than work. There is a certain&nbsp;
amount of privilege attached to being able to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:42.480
that. Living costs have to be covered, families&nbsp;
need support, futures must be secured. But perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:48.080
that is precisely the point - that in a fairer&nbsp;
world, everyone should be able to say that.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:53.600
The pandemic isn't over, so it's hard to say how&nbsp;
the world has permanently changed. But it's even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:59.200
harder to imagine things going back to the way&nbsp;
they were. People are simply much more aware now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:05.040
that work is about the people who do it - and much&nbsp;
more conscious about the systems that govern it.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:10.800
We have the opportunity to change almost&nbsp;
everything when it comes to how we arrange work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.520
Let's do it in a way that's fair and equitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:19.040
for everyone and fix some of the systemic&nbsp;
problems we had before the pandemic.

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:22.880
And that’s it for this edition of&nbsp;
business beyond. If you enjoyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:26.320
our video, don’t forget to check out our&nbsp;
other content. And while you’re at it, hit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:35.921
like and subscribe. Thanks&nbsp;
for watching and see you soon!

